      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "title: \"Mini-project CAM C301 Week 4 & 5: Applying NLP for Topic Modelling\"\n",
        "author: \"Rodrigo Grandy, PhD\"\n",
        "date: \"2025-07-27\" \n",
        "execute:\n",
        "  dir: . # set the working directory to the folder where the .qmd file is located\n",
        "format:\n",
        "    pdf:\n",
        "      documentclass: extarticle\n",
        "      toc: true\n",
        "      title-block-banner: false \n",
        "      mainfont: Helvetica\n",
        "      sansfont: Helvetica\n",
        "      fontsize: 14pt\n",
        "      fig-format: retina\n",
        "      fig-dpi: 300\n",
        "      fig-align: center\n",
        "      number-sections: true\n",
        "      colorlinks: false\n",
        "      geometry: \"left=2cm,right=2cm,top=3cm,bottom=3cm\"\n",
        "      include-before-body: before-body.tex\n",
        "      include-in-header: header.tex\n",
        "header-includes:\n",
        "  - \\usepackage{float}\n",
        "  - \\usepackage{adjustbox}\n",
        "  - \\usepackage{booktabs}\n",
        "  \n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "# Introduction\n",
        "\n",
        "In this report we present a comprehensive time series analysis of book sales data from Nielsen BookScan, focusing on sales forecasting for independent publishers. The analysis examines historical sales patterns for two prominent titles: \"The Very Hungry Caterpillar\" and \"The Alchemist,\" using data from 2012 onwards. Multiple forecasting methodologies are implemented and compared, including classical ARIMA models, machine learning approaches (light XGBoost), deep learning techniques (LSTM), and hybrid model combinations. The objective is to develop accurate sales forecasts to support publishers' inventory management, stock control, and investment decisions. Performance evaluation utilizes statistical metrics including Mean Absolute Error (MAE) and Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE) to assess forecasting accuracy across different methodological approaches.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "# Objectives\n",
        "\n",
        "- Data Preprocessing: Clean and resample the sales data to create consistent weekly intervals for time series analysis.\n",
        "- Time Series Pattern Analysis: Decompose sales data and test stationarity to understand seasonal trends and temporal characteristics.\n",
        "- Apply Auto ARIMA models to forecast sales periods for both selected books.\n",
        "- Implement XGBoost or lightGBM and LSTM models with hyperparameter tuning for sales forecasting.\n",
        "- Hybrid Approaches: Combine SARIMA-LSTM models and compare weekly versus monthly prediction strategies.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "# Methods\n",
        "\n",
        "The analysis utilized Python 3.11 with key libraries including pandas, numpy, statsmodels, scikit-learn, LightGBM, TensorFlow/Keras, and sktime for comprehensive time series forecasting on Nielsen BookScan data (2012-2024). Data preprocessing involved loading four Excel tabs, converting ISBNs to strings, establishing datetime indices, and resampling to weekly intervals (W-SAT frequency) with zero-filling for missing periods. Post-2012 data filtering focused on \"The Alchemist\" and \"The Very Hungry Caterpillar\" for detailed analysis.\n",
        "\n",
        "Statistical preprocessing included stationarity testing (ADF, KPSS), ACF/PACF analysis, and STL decomposition. Classical forecasting employed Auto-ARIMA with seasonal parameters (m=52). Machine learning implementation featured LightGBM with recursive windowing, incorporating TransformedTargetForecaster for deseasonalization and detrending. Deep learning utilized multi-step LSTM architectures with MinMax scaling and various preprocessing methods. Hybrid models combined SARIMA-LSTM in sequential/parallel configurations. Performance evaluation used 32-week out-of-sample forecasts, assessed via MAE and MAPE across weekly and monthly aggregations.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "```{=latex}\n",
        "\\begin{figure}[H]\n",
        "\\centering\n",
        "\\begin{subfigure}[t]{1\\linewidth}\n",
        "\\includegraphics[\n",
        "  width=\\linewidth,\n",
        "  trim=0 0 0 0,  % Adjust this value if black borders are present\n",
        "  clip\n",
        "]{figures/training_test_both_books.png.png}\n",
        "\\end{subfigure}\n",
        "\\caption{Word Frequency Analysis for All Reviews in UK Locations form Google Reviews and Trustpilot: Word frequency analysis of customer reviews collected from PureGym locations across the UK, using data sourced from both Google Reviews and Trustpilot. The figure visualizes the most common words appearing in customer feedback after text preprocessing steps such as the removal of stop words, numbers, symbols, and gym-specific terms (e.g., \"pure\", \"gym\"). The analysis encompasses both positive and negative reviews, providing a holistic view of the language most frequently used by customers.}\n",
        "\\label{fig:elbow_panel}\n",
        "\\end{figure}\n",
        "\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "# Results\n",
        "\n",
        "Following data preprocessing and word frequency analysis, the top 10 most frequent words showed a prevalence of positive terms (great, clean, good, friendly), aligning with 70.81% and 68.53% of reviews scoring 4-5 stars on Google and Trustpilot respectively. However, neutral descriptors like \"equipment\" and \"staff\" among frequent terms highlighted limitations of this basic frequency analysis in capturing contextual sentiment (See figure 1 and notebook 1). \n",
        "\n",
        "This finding motivated implementation of advanced NLP techniques including topic modeling and emotion classification. To better understand negative sentiment while optimizing computing resources, subsequent analysis focused on reviews scoring below 3 from locations present in both datasets. Figure 2 provides a visual representation of where these reviews were most concentrated, highlighting potential areas for operational improvement.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "```{=latex}\n",
        "\\begin{figure}[H]\n",
        "\\centering\n",
        "\\begin{subfigure}[t]{0.6\\linewidth}\n",
        "\\includegraphics[\n",
        "  width=\\linewidth,\n",
        "  trim=0 80 0 0,  % Adjust this value if black borders are present\n",
        "  clip\n",
        "]{figures/negative_review_top_30_locations_map_uk_goog_trustpilot.png}\n",
        "\\end{subfigure}\n",
        "\\caption{Distribution of Top 30 Locations with the Highest Negative Review Counts from Google Reviews and Trustpilot: The figure shows the distribution of the locations with the highest counts of negative reviews. Following the rubric instructions, the top 20 locations with the highest number of negative reviews were selected for each dataset. Next, these lists were concatenated and the top 30 locations with the highest number of negative reviews were selected.The size of each symbol corresponds to the number of negative reviews at that location, highlighting geographic hotspots of customer dissatisfaction.}\n",
        "\\label{fig:elbow_panel}\n",
        "\\end{figure}\n",
        "\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Following text preprocessing, negative customer reviews underwent topic modeling using BERTopic to extract dominant thematic patterns. The analysis focused on examining topic distributions between all negative reviews from shared locations versus reviews from the top 30 locations with highest complaint volumes. Word cloud visualization revealed consistent high frequency of terms such as \"equipment,\" \"clean,\" \"staff,\" \"weights,\" and \"machines,\" indicating systematic concerns regarding facility maintenance and service delivery. Importantly, minimal differences were observed between the two subsets, suggesting that negative sentiment drivers are globally distributed rather than location-specific phenomena (Figure 3). This observation was confirmed by frequency distribution analysis, which revealed that equipment-related, cleanliness, and staff-related terms appeared amongst the most frequent terms used in both negative reviews subsets (Figure 4). \n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "```{=latex}\n",
        "\\begin{figure}[H]\n",
        "\\centering\n",
        "\\begin{subfigure}[t]{1\\linewidth}\n",
        "\\includegraphics[\n",
        "  width=\\linewidth,\n",
        "  trim=0 0 0 0,  % Adjust this value if black borders are present\n",
        "  clip\n",
        "]{figures/wordcloud_google_trustp_neg_reviews_vs_t30n.png}\n",
        "\\end{subfigure}\n",
        "\\caption{Comparison of the Most Frequent Topics Themes Among the top 30 Gyms with the Highest Negative Reviews versus All Negative Reviews from Shared Locations: Word cloud derived from negative customer reviews at the top 30 PureGym locations with the highest complaint volumes, combining data from both Google Reviews and Trustpilot. The word cloud was generated after rigorous text preprocessing, including removal of stop words, numbers, gym-specific terms (“pure”, “gym”), and short words, to isolate meaningful terms relevant to customer dissatisfaction.}\n",
        "\\label{fig:elbow_panel}\n",
        "\\end{figure}\n",
        "\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Analysis of emotion distribution across negative reviews from the 30 highest-complaint PureGym locations using a BERT-based classifier revealed predominant emotions of anger and sadness (Figure 5). However, joy emerged as one of the most prevalent emotions, which contradicts expectations for negative reviews and suggests potential model misclassification or need for fine-tuning. The presence of anger and sadness indicates that customer dissatisfaction extends beyond logistical complaints, reflecting significant emotional impact from operational failures. These findings highlight the importance of model optimization before drawing conclusions about the relationship between complaint themes and customer sentiment.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "```{=latex}\n",
        "\\begin{figure}[H]\n",
        "\\centering\n",
        "\\begin{subfigure}[t]{1\\linewidth}\n",
        "\\includegraphics[\n",
        "  width=\\linewidth,\n",
        "  trim=0 0 0 0,  % Adjust this value if black borders are present\n",
        "  clip\n",
        "]{figures/word_freq_t30n_vs_all_neg_comm_loc.png}\n",
        "\\end{subfigure}\n",
        "\\caption{Comparison of Topic Word Frequencies: Top 30 Highest-Volume Negative Review Gyms vs. All Negative Reviews from Same Locations; Blue bars represent frequency words for all negative reviews from common locations combined. Orange bars represent frequency words for the locations with the to 30 highest numbers of negative reviews}\n",
        "\\label{fig:elbow_panel}\n",
        "\\end{figure}\n",
        "\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "```{=latex}\n",
        "\\begin{figure}[H]\n",
        "\\centering\n",
        "\\begin{subfigure}[t]{0.7\\linewidth}\n",
        "\\includegraphics[\n",
        "  width=\\linewidth,\n",
        "  trim=0 0 0 0,  % Adjust this value if black borders are present\n",
        "  clip\n",
        "]{figures/top_30_negative_reviews_emotions.png}\n",
        "\\end{subfigure}\n",
        "\\caption{Distribution of emotional categories in negative customer reviews for the 30 PureGym locations with the highest complaint volumes, as classified by a BERT-based emotion model. Data from both Google Reviews and Trustpilot are combined to illustrate the prevalence of specific negative emotions driving customer dissatisfaction across the UK network.}\n",
        "\\label{fig:elbow_panel}\n",
        "\\end{figure}\n",
        "\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Comparison of emotion distribution between Google Reviews and Trustpilot revealed consistent patterns across platforms, with anger and sadness dominating negative reviews (Figure 6). This alignment indicates that customer dissatisfaction reflects systemic operational issues rather than platform-specific biases, validating the robustness of our sentiment analysis methodology. However, joy consistently appeared in negative reviews from both platforms, suggesting potential model misclassification requiring further tuning or data preprocessing. The cross-platform consistency strengthens confidence in identifying core operational issues while highlighting the need for model optimization before implementing targeted interventions based on these emotional insights.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "```{=latex}\n",
        "\\begin{figure}[H]\n",
        "\\centering\n",
        "\\begin{subfigure}[t]{0.8\\linewidth}\n",
        "\\includegraphics[\n",
        "  width=\\linewidth,\n",
        "  trim=0 0 0 0,  % Adjust this value if black borders are present\n",
        "  clip\n",
        "]{figures/comparison_negative_reviews_emotions_by_platform.png}\n",
        "\\end{subfigure}\n",
        "\\caption{Emotional distribution of negative customer reviews for the 30 PureGym locations with the highest complaint volumes, combining data from Google Reviews and Trustpilot as classified by a BERT-based emotion model.}\n",
        "\\label{fig:elbow_panel}\n",
        "\\end{figure}\n",
        "\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Finally, we explored the capacity of a small LLM to perform sentiment analysis and generate actionable insights. Table 1 demonstrates how microsoft/Phi-4-mini-instruct synthesizes granular customer concerns into prioritized operational recommendations, transforming topics like \"free weights availability\" and \"hygiene and cleanliness\" into concrete business actions. While BERTopic excels at transparent thematic clustering, the LLM adds strategic value by generating context-aware, multi-step recommendations directly from extracted topics. However, it is important to consider that this efficiency comes at the cost of reduced transparency and higher computational requirements. \n",
        "\n",
        "In this context, the combination of LLM-based insight extraction with traditional topic modeling provides a sensible strategy for both analytical depth and actionable clarity for implementing effective operational changes.\n"
      ],
      "id": "59cfb054"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: false\n",
        "#| output: asis\n",
        "\n",
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "import ast\n",
        "import re\n",
        "\n",
        "# Load your data\n",
        "df = pd.read_csv('review_analysis_llm_insights.csv').head(5)\n",
        "\n",
        "def process_column_data(data):\n",
        "    \"\"\"Convert string lists to cleaned text with line breaks\"\"\"\n",
        "    if isinstance(data, str) and data.startswith('['):\n",
        "        # Convert string representation to actual list\n",
        "        items = ast.literal_eval(data)\n",
        "    elif isinstance(data, list):\n",
        "        items = data\n",
        "    else:\n",
        "        items = [data]\n",
        "    # Remove all extra whitespace and normalize, then strip\n",
        "    cleaned_items = []\n",
        "    for item in items:\n",
        "        # Split, rejoin to normalize spacing, then strip whitespace\n",
        "        clean_text = ' '.join(str(item).split()).strip()\n",
        "        if clean_text:\n",
        "            cleaned_items.append(clean_text)\n",
        "    return cleaned_items\n",
        "\n",
        "# Process the data\n",
        "df['original_topics'] = df['original_topics'].apply(process_column_data)\n",
        "df['compressed_insights'] = df['compressed_insights'].apply(process_column_data)\n",
        "\n",
        "def latex_escape(text):\n",
        "    \"\"\"Escape LaTeX special characters\"\"\"\n",
        "    replacements = {\n",
        "        '\\\\': r'\\textbackslash{}', \n",
        "        '&': r'\\&', \n",
        "        '%': r'\\%', \n",
        "        '$': r'\\$', \n",
        "        '#': r'\\#', \n",
        "        '_': r'\\_', \n",
        "        '{': r'\\{', \n",
        "        '}': r'\\}', \n",
        "        '~': r'\\textasciitilde{}', \n",
        "        '^': r'\\textasciicircum{}',\n",
        "    }\n",
        "    for char, escape in replacements.items():\n",
        "        text = text.replace(char, escape)\n",
        "    return text\n",
        "\n",
        "# Format for LaTeX with proper spacing\n",
        "df['Original Topics'] = df['original_topics'].apply(\n",
        "    lambda x: r'\\newline '.join([latex_escape(item.strip()) for item in x if item.strip()])\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "df['Compressed Insights'] = df['compressed_insights'].apply(\n",
        "    lambda x: r'\\newline '.join([latex_escape(item.strip()) for item in x if item.strip()])\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "# Clean up any potential leading/trailing whitespace\n",
        "df['Original Topics'] = df['Original Topics'].str.strip()\n",
        "df['Compressed Insights'] = df['Compressed Insights'].str.strip()\n",
        "\n",
        "# Select only the columns we want to display\n",
        "df = df[['Original Topics', 'Compressed Insights']]\n",
        "\n",
        "# Generate LaTeX table\n",
        "latex_table = df.to_latex(\n",
        "    index=False,\n",
        "    escape=False,\n",
        "    column_format='p{5cm}p{11cm}',\n",
        "    longtable=False\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "# Add vertical space between rows\n",
        "latex_table = latex_table.replace(r'\\\\' + '\\n', r'\\\\[0.5em]' + '\\n')\n",
        "\n",
        "# Final table formatting\n",
        "final_table = f\"\"\"\\\\begin{{landscape}}\n",
        "\\\\usepackage{{booktabs}}\n",
        "\\\\renewcommand{{\\\\arraystretch}}{{1.2}}\n",
        "\\\\begin{{table}}\n",
        "\\\\centering\n",
        "{latex_table}\n",
        "\\\\caption{{LLM-generated insights from negative PureGym customer feedback. The table demonstrates how large language model analysis transforms detailed customer concerns into prioritized, actionable recommendations for operational improvement.}}\n",
        "\\\\label{{tbl-topics_insights}}\n",
        "\\\\end{{table}}\n",
        "\\\\end{{landscape}}\"\"\"\n",
        "\n",
        "print(final_table)"
      ],
      "id": "d58e627a",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
